window.MTH105_INDEX=[{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"From data to decisions","x":"Statistics is the science of collecting, organizing, analyzing, interpreting and communicating data to support decisions. Topics: Descriptive and inferential statistics · Definitions of representative samples and importance of sample size, and impact of outliers · Illustrative practical examples of use of Statistics. CLO 1."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s1","n":"Descriptive vs inferential","t":"Descriptive and inferential statistics","x":"Descriptive statistics describe the data we have: tables, graphs, averages. Inferential statistics use a sample to estimate or test something about the whole population."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s2","n":"Population and sample","t":"Population vs sample","x":"The population is the whole group we want to study (size N). A sample is the part we actually measure (size n). Numbers that describe a population are parameters; numbers from a sample are statistics."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s3","n":"Types of data","t":"Types of data","x":"Qualitative data are categories: nominal (no order) or ordinal (ordered). Quantitative data are numbers: discrete (counted) or continuous (measured)."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s4","n":"Sort the variables","t":"Activity · Classify the variables","x":"Pick the type for each variable."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s5","n":"Sample size","t":"Representative samples and sample size","x":"A representative sample looks like the population: choose it at random, not by convenience. Bigger random samples give estimates closer to the truth."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s6","n":"Outliers","t":"The impact of outliers","x":"An outlier is a value far from the rest. It pulls the mean towards it; the median barely moves."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s7","n":"Check the claim","t":"Activity · Is this conclusion valid?","x":"A campus newsletter polls 30 students in the main library at 9 PM: 21 say they prefer studying at night. Headline: “70% of students prefer studying at night.”"},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s8","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"New examples. Answer first, then check."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s9","n":"Quick check","t":"Quick check","x":"Calculate, then open the answer."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s10","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next week: Ch.2, Picturing Variation with Graphs."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Organize before you analyze","x":"Raw data are hard to read. Tables and graphs show the pattern. Topics: Frequency distributions · Histograms and frequency polygons · Cumulative frequency distributions. CLO 1."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s1","n":"Relative frequency","t":"Frequency and relative frequency","x":"Count each category (f), then divide by the total (n) to get the share."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s2","n":"Bar and pie","t":"Bar charts and pie charts","x":"For categories: a bar chart compares sizes (bars with gaps); a pie chart shows parts of a whole (100%)."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s3","n":"Class width","t":"Grouping numbers into classes","x":"For many numbers, group them into classes of equal width. Width = range ÷ number of classes, rounded up."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s4","n":"Frequency table","t":"Frequency distribution and histogram","x":"Build the table: class, boundaries, midpoint, frequency, relative and cumulative frequency. A histogram draws it with touching bars."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s5","n":"Shape","t":"The shape of a histogram","x":"Follow the tail. Tail to the right: positively skewed. Tail to the left: negatively skewed. No long tail: symmetric."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s6","n":"Polygon and ogive","t":"Frequency polygons and cumulative frequency","x":"A frequency polygon joins the midpoints and closes on the axis. An ogive plots cumulative frequency at each upper boundary."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s7","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"New data. Answer on paper first."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s8","n":"Quick check","t":"Quick check","x":"Calculate, then open the answer."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s9","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Week 3, Ch.3: Numerical Summaries of Center and Variation."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"One number for the centre","x":"Where is the middle of the data, and where does a value sit? Topics: Mean, median, and mode · Percentiles. CLO 1."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s1","n":"Mean","t":"The mean","x":"The mean is the balance point: add all values, divide by how many there are."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s2","n":"Median and mode","t":"Median and mode","x":"Median: the middle value after sorting, at position (n + 1) ÷ 2. Mode: the most frequent value, the only centre for nominal data."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s3","n":"Which centre?","t":"Mean, median, mode and skew","x":"Outliers and skew pull the mean towards the tail. For skewed data, report the median."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s4","n":"Percentiles and quartiles","t":"Percentiles and quartiles","x":"A percentile tells you the position of a value: P25 = Q1, P50 = median, P75 = Q3. Sort the data, find the median, then the median of each half."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s5","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Order the data first."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s6","n":"Quick check","t":"Quick check","x":"Calculate, then open the answer."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s7","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Week 4, measures of dispersion."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"One number for the spread","x":"The same mean can hide very different spreads. Topics: Variance and standard deviation · Coefficient of variation. CLO 1."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s1","n":"Range, IQR, boxplot","t":"Range, IQR and the boxplot","x":"Range = max − min. IQR = Q3 − Q1, the spread of the middle 50%. The boxplot shows min, Q1, median, Q3 and max."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s2","n":"Variance","t":"Sample variance","x":"Variance is the average squared distance from the mean. For a sample, divide by n − 1."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s3","n":"Standard deviation","t":"Standard deviation","x":"The standard deviation is the square root of the variance: the typical distance from the mean, in the original units."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s4","n":"CV","t":"Coefficient of variation","x":"The CV is the SD as a percentage of the mean. Use it to compare spread across different means or units."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s5","n":"Check any data","t":"Tool · Check your calculations","x":"Type any data set to check the mean, median, mode, s², s and CV step by step."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s6","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Use a deviation table. Check with the tool."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s7","n":"Quick check","t":"Quick check","x":"Calculate, then open the answer."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s8","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Week 5, Ch 5: Modeling Variation with Probability."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"From describing to predicting","x":"Weeks 1–4 described data we have. Probability measures how likely something is before it happens: a number from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain). Topics: Definitions of events and sample space · Operations on probabilities. CLO 2."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s1","n":"Sample space and events","t":"Experiment, outcome, sample space, event","x":"An experiment has uncertain results. Each result is an outcome. All outcomes form the sample space S. An event is a set of outcomes we care about."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s2","n":"Basic rule","t":"The basic rule","x":"When outcomes are equally likely, count: favourable outcomes over all outcomes. Every probability is between 0 and 1, and all outcomes add up to 1."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s3","n":"Complement","t":"The complement rule","x":"“Not A” is everything outside A. The two always add to 1."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s4","n":"Addition rule","t":"The addition rule and Venn diagrams","x":"“A or B” = at least one happens. Subtract the overlap so it is not counted twice. If A and B are mutually exclusive (no overlap), just add."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s5","n":"Multiplication rule","t":"The multiplication rule and tree diagrams","x":"For independent events, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). In a tree, multiply along a path; add paths for “or”."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s6","n":"Tree diagram","t":"Tree diagrams with two equal branches","x":"Each step splits into two equally likely options, so every branch is 1/2. List the sample space from the ends of the paths, then multiply along a path: 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s7","n":"Types of events","t":"Activity · Name the relationship","x":"Pick the relationship between the two events."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s8","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Name the rule first, then calculate."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s9","n":"Quick check","t":"Quick check","x":"Calculate, then open the answer."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s10","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Week 6, discrete probability distributions."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Every value, with its probability","x":"A random variable X turns outcomes into numbers. A discrete one takes countable values (0, 1, 2 …). Its distribution lists every value with its probability. Topics: Definition of discrete random variables · Expected value, variance and standard deviation of discrete random variables. CLO 2."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#s1","n":"Valid distribution","t":"A valid probability distribution","x":"Two conditions: every P(x) is between 0 and 1, and all P(x) add up to 1. A missing value k = 1 − (sum of the others)."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#s2","n":"Expected value","t":"Expected value E(X)","x":"E(X) is the long-run average of X: multiply each value by its probability and add."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#s3","n":"Variance and SD","t":"Variance and standard deviation of X","x":"Var(X) measures how far X typically lands from E(X). SD = √Var(X)."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#s4","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Check validity first."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#s5","n":"Quick check","t":"Quick check","x":"Calculate, then open the answer."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#s6","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":"Next: Week 7, Bernoulli and Binomial distributions."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Five parts, 50 exercises","x":"Work through the five parts in order. Answer on paper, then open the model answer. Method: as covered in class and in last Wednesday’s lab revision."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s1","n":"1 · Draw graphs","t":"Part 1A · Draw the graph","x":"Histogram: boundaries on the x-axis, touching bars. Polygon: midpoints, closed on the axis. Boxplot: min, Q1, median, Q3, max."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s2","n":"1 · Read graphs","t":"Part 1B · Read the graph","x":"Read the values first, then calculate."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s3","n":"2 · Centre","t":"Part 2A · Central tendency","x":"Mean, median, mode. Sort the data before the median."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s4","n":"2 · Spread","t":"Part 2B · Measures of variation","x":"Range, IQR, variance, SD, CV. For s²: subtract, square, add, divide by n − 1."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s5","n":"3 · The rules","t":"Part 3 · Probability rules","x":"AND means multiply. OR means add; if BOTH is given, subtract it once. NOT means 1 minus."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s6","n":"3 · Simple and NOT","t":"Part 3A · Simple probability and complement","x":"Count favourable over total. For NOT, subtract from 1."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s7","n":"3 · OR","t":"Part 3B · Addition rule (OR)","x":"No overlap: just add. BOTH given: add, then subtract BOTH. Fill the Venn diagram from the middle out."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s8","n":"3 · AND","t":"Part 3C · Multiplication rule (AND)","x":"Independent and fair: every choice of two is 1/2. Multiply."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s9","n":"4 · Tree diagrams","t":"Part 4 · Tree diagrams","x":"Every step splits into two equal branches of 1/2. Multiply along a path; add paths for OR."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s10","n":"5 · Valid and k","t":"Part 5A · Valid distribution and k","x":"Every P(x) between 0 and 1, and the sum equals 1."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s11","n":"5 · E, Var, SD","t":"Part 5B · E(X), Var(X) and SD","x":"E(X) = Σx·P(x). Var(X) = Σ(x − μ)²·P(x). SD = √Var(X). Interpret in one sentence."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#s12","n":"Exam day","t":"Exam day","x":"Monday 19 October 2026, 9:30 AM, HUB-Yard Zones A, B, D. Bring your ID and a standard calculator. Good luck."}];
